1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:06,500
Anthropic just warned us that it could be approaching a major threshold for AI improving AI.

2
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:15,940
And now, an independent report from Meter Research gives us a look at what's happening behind the scenes, including results from clawed models that are unreleased.

3
00:00:16,430 --> 00:00:24,900
Meanwhile, OpenAI has a new model rated critical in cybersecurity, and AI is continuing to help researchers make more scientific discoveries.

4
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:31,680
But after this week's US elections, the pushback against AI and data centers is getting harder to ignore.

5
00:00:31,918 --> 00:00:36,932
AI stocks are getting hammered, and there's growing calls to slow this whole thing down.

6
00:00:37,280 --> 00:00:40,932
So, how fast is AI actually accelerating?

7
00:00:41,200 --> 00:00:44,400
And how far will governments go to slow it down?

8
00:00:44,686 --> 00:00:45,360
Let's get into it.

9
00:00:45,614 --> 00:00:49,920
Alright, so Anthropic just updated its IPO filing this week.

10
00:00:50,080 --> 00:00:57,892
And in it, over on page 29, they added this new paragraph about RSI, recursive self-improvement.

11
00:00:58,158 --> 00:01:14,660
They wrote, Much of our evidence regarding the pace of such AI-driven acceleration is derived from lagging indicators, and our ability to forecast or measure the rate at which our capabilities are advancing, including any acceleration attributable to our most recent or unreleased models, is limited.

12
00:01:14,894 --> 00:01:26,436
As a result, we have in the past not identified, and may in the future not identify, a significant acceleration in our rate of development until after it has occurred.

13
00:01:26,702 --> 00:01:41,444
For example, our internal estimates and our expectations regarding future models rather than our current models indicate that we may imminently meet a responsible scaling policy's definition of dramatically accelerated progress attributable to AI.

14
00:01:41,760 --> 00:01:49,508
So basically, they're talking about AI helping them build better AI here, fast enough to trigger one of their own safety thresholds.

15
00:01:49,838 --> 00:01:58,916
They haven't declared that any current or unreleased models have crossed it, but they also warn that they might only recognize it's been crossed after it's been crossed.

16
00:01:59,278 --> 00:02:00,932
This could then force them to slow down.

17
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:06,960
And that brings us to the new meter report that gives us a better look at what's actually going on.

18
00:02:07,246 --> 00:02:14,560
So the research group meter has done their own independent assessment of anthropics AI RD acceleration in the month of October.

19
00:02:14,720 --> 00:02:24,080
And what they've found is that benchmark progress has been roughly 1.9 times faster since last year in October 2025.

20
00:02:24,398 --> 00:02:28,480
Think roughly two years of progress at the old pace happening in one year.

21
00:02:28,654 --> 00:02:31,200
That's about the rate of improvement now, according to them.

22
00:02:31,470 --> 00:02:43,300
They actually say these new internal models, the ones highlighted in orange that we're going to talk more about shortly, make a permanent slope change look more plausible than a one-time jump at Mythos preview.

23
00:02:43,520 --> 00:02:47,840
Meaning essentially this new rate of growth might just be the norm now.

24
00:02:48,046 --> 00:02:49,412
And here's where it gets interesting.

25
00:02:49,680 --> 00:03:02,480
Again, this is including internal models we haven't had access to, such as Model 3, which finished training in August, September, and even Model 4, which finished in September, October.

26
00:03:02,702 --> 00:03:15,044
I remember when Anthropic vaguely talked about Model 2 in one of their recent posts, but I guess now we've already moved on to Model 3 and Model 4, these, of course, being placeholder names for their next generation models.

27
00:03:15,310 --> 00:03:21,700
They actually don't give us much detail about Model 3 in this report, which you can imagine is their next model being released.

28
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:31,108
But here they have a whole section on Model 4, which apparently scored above 50% on all 15 benchmarks used for its capability index.

29
00:03:31,374 --> 00:03:39,120
You can see here in the graph, it's a little bit messy, but model four is on the far right, and each of these darker colored lines represents a different benchmark.

30
00:03:39,310 --> 00:03:42,880
Look at where those lines were for Opus 4.6, just nine months earlier.

31
00:03:43,040 --> 00:03:44,720
A bunch were still below 50%.

32
00:03:45,280 --> 00:03:51,844
Now though, every one of these 15 tests is above it, which I think is the first time this has ever happened.

33
00:03:52,110 --> 00:03:55,840
Employees also reported roughly 12 times the productivity with it.

34
00:03:56,238 --> 00:03:56,900
12 times.

35
00:03:57,230 --> 00:03:57,892
That is insane.

36
00:03:58,158 --> 00:04:05,668
You can see this internal metric has already been skyrocketing, but now it's skyrocketing even more, somehow, seemingly exponentially.

37
00:04:05,998 --> 00:04:09,828
So you're probably wondering, what's actually causing all this?

38
00:04:10,256 --> 00:04:18,736
Well, unsurprisingly, meter tentatively attributes about 80% of the current acceleration directly to AI.

39
00:04:18,926 --> 00:04:34,468
In fact, they even suggest the acceleration in late 2025 was likely influenced by non-AI factors, but that the acceleration now, in October 2026, is again definitely mostly AI related, which I thought was pretty interesting.

40
00:04:34,896 --> 00:04:38,656
Now, does this mean recursive self-improvement is here?

41
00:04:38,896 --> 00:04:46,496
I mean, personally, I'd say yes, at least the beginnings of it, just based off of everything we've been seeing towards the end of this year.

42
00:04:46,576 --> 00:04:47,492
And now this.

43
00:04:47,822 --> 00:04:59,204
But if you ask Anthropic, their policy actually allows for a warning threshold based on dramatically accelerated AI development, even before models can replace its entire research staff.

44
00:04:59,534 --> 00:05:11,332
So humans can still be involved while AI starts making the process of building better AI much faster, obviously, which is pretty much exactly what's happening now, hence the updates to their IPO filing.

45
00:05:11,566 --> 00:05:19,616
There's still, of course, crucial stages of training where the models aren't making major contributions autonomously, that humans are still very much a part of.

46
00:05:19,886 --> 00:05:27,172
But Meter also says they haven't really identified a convincing benchmark yet that would stop this acceleration over the next year.

47
00:05:27,536 --> 00:05:28,416
So yeah.

48
00:05:28,750 --> 00:05:31,536
To put it simply, they expect this to continue.

49
00:05:31,696 --> 00:05:35,456
And remember, we're only seeing part of the picture anyways.

50
00:05:35,758 --> 00:05:42,736
Anthropic apparently redacted some of the most informative evidence behind this assessment, including a forecast involving a future model.

51
00:05:42,926 --> 00:05:47,428
So there's information feeding into these conclusions that we just don't get to see yet.

52
00:05:47,758 --> 00:05:50,256
Which brings us back to that warning in the S1.

53
00:05:50,478 --> 00:05:55,812
Anthropic said it might only recognize a significant acceleration after it's already happened.

54
00:05:56,046 --> 00:05:56,896
That's the key thing.

55
00:05:57,198 --> 00:06:04,580
So if the model is helping build the next generation keep getting better, how much faster does this get before we even realize what's happening?

56
00:06:04,896 --> 00:06:08,164
Or before Anthropic even realizes what's happening.

57
00:06:08,430 --> 00:06:17,776
I mean, the only reason we're even finding out about this, about these unreleased internal models, model three and model four, is because of meter research.

58
00:06:17,936 --> 00:06:20,900
I believe this is the first time they've ever even been publicly mentioned.

59
00:06:21,216 --> 00:06:30,096
Now, speaking of AI improving AI, there was another experiment this week where an AI actually edited another AI's model weights to make it better.

60
00:06:30,318 --> 00:06:34,660
I already made a full video on this yesterday, but let me quickly show you what happened.

61
00:06:34,976 --> 00:06:46,084
So basically, researchers at MIT gave GBT6 Astra access to a small Quen model and asked it to make it better at math without running a normal training process.

62
00:06:46,382 --> 00:06:51,460
And Astra found an issue that was causing the model to get stuck in reasoning loops.

63
00:06:51,696 --> 00:07:04,548
It then edited just 0.016% of the weights, and the model score on a math benchmark jumped from 7.9% to 48.1% using the same sampling settings.

64
00:07:04,942 --> 00:07:12,548
So it changed just a tiny fraction of the actual numbers or weights inside the model and substantially improved how it performed.

65
00:07:12,782 --> 00:07:15,296
This is not something LLMs were able to do before.

66
00:07:15,470 --> 00:07:17,776
And other people have already been trying similar things.

67
00:07:18,158 --> 00:07:31,716
Lagora's CTO, for example, says he got Fable to write the weights for a small neural network that distinguishes real from fake legal citations, basically creating a working network directly, which he says initially beat his trained version.

68
00:07:31,936 --> 00:07:36,260
So there's a lot more to these experiments, and I'll link my full breakdown if you missed it.

69
00:07:36,496 --> 00:07:41,536
But yeah, this is another approach to AI building and improving AI.

70
00:07:41,678 --> 00:07:46,416
And by extension, understanding AI that I'm definitely keeping an eye on.

71
00:07:46,880 --> 00:07:56,068
Now, going back to the topic of models we don't have access to yet, OpenAI also quietly showed us a new internal model of their own this week.

72
00:07:56,334 --> 00:07:59,556
This was in a post about using AI for physical engineering.

73
00:07:59,822 --> 00:08:09,156
And down here, they compare it with GBT6 Astra on BenchCAD 2.0, which tests things like reconstructing 3D parts and figuring out connections in circuit boards.

74
00:08:09,454 --> 00:08:14,480
As you can see, Astra at its highest reasoning setting scores 0.594.

75
00:08:14,720 --> 00:08:18,240
This newer model, though, scores 0.875.

76
00:08:18,670 --> 00:08:20,160
That's a pretty substantial jump.

77
00:08:20,320 --> 00:08:23,760
They don't name the model here, but we can speculate on what it might be.

78
00:08:23,950 --> 00:08:28,720
I'm guessing this is GBT Aeon, which we've been hearing quite a bit about recently.

79
00:08:28,942 --> 00:08:32,560
In this same post, they show that they've already been using Astra for design.

80
00:08:32,878 --> 00:08:38,788
Two employees actually designed this little device for talking to Codex in under a week with it, as just a side project.

81
00:08:38,990 --> 00:08:42,080
The model handled the enclosure, circuit board, and software.

82
00:08:42,382 --> 00:08:49,636
Getting it ready for manufacturing still took review and iteration, but a manufacturer produced prototypes in under a month, which is pretty fast.

83
00:08:49,998 --> 00:09:01,760
So we're already seeing AI help turn an idea into an actual physical object, while the next internal model OpenAI has is already scoring considerably higher on doing exactly that.

84
00:09:02,062 --> 00:09:06,948
You can imagine this is great news for OpenAI, especially with their upcoming hardware device.

85
00:09:07,374 --> 00:09:15,760
Now, OpenAI also released something else this week that takes us straight back to the security side of all this: GBT 6 cyber.

86
00:09:15,982 --> 00:09:24,228
They classify it as critical in cybersecurity under their preparedness framework, up from high for GBT 5.6 cyber.

87
00:09:24,462 --> 00:09:33,200
This is a version of Astra specifically trained for cybersecurity work, finding vulnerabilities, developing working exploits, and testing systems with permission.

88
00:09:33,390 --> 00:09:36,960
And Astra apparently already had that critical designation too.

89
00:09:37,230 --> 00:09:40,800
Cyber just takes those capabilities and specializes them further.

90
00:09:41,006 --> 00:09:51,920
On Exploit Gym, for example, where the model has to exploit a specified vulnerability, it scores 48.5% compared with 32.1% for the previous cyber model.

91
00:09:52,080 --> 00:09:56,612
I believe only Gemini 4 Carbon beats this with a score of like 54 or something.

92
00:09:56,910 --> 00:09:59,280
But the real world results are what stood out to me.

93
00:09:59,566 --> 00:10:09,700
OpenAI says it found 81 previously unknown high severity vulnerabilities in Firefox, even after extensive testing with earlier Frontier models.

94
00:10:10,000 --> 00:10:23,840
And in Linux, their pipeline produced 948 working local privilege escalation exploits, meaning someone who already has limited access to a machine could potentially use one of these flaws to gain full administrator access.

95
00:10:24,110 --> 00:10:26,480
They also generated candidate fixes for review.

96
00:10:26,702 --> 00:10:31,012
The run cost about $1.2 million in API usage.

97
00:10:31,246 --> 00:10:32,996
So this was a serious research effort.

98
00:10:33,294 --> 00:10:38,148
Of course, you also need this thing to stick to the systems it's actually allowed to test.

99
00:10:38,478 --> 00:10:40,324
You don't want another hugging face incident.

100
00:10:40,560 --> 00:10:51,652
In one evaluation, they deliberately put easy targets outside its authorized scope, and with its deployment safety instructions, it stayed within bounds in every tested case, which is a good sign.

101
00:10:51,854 --> 00:10:58,500
And unsurprisingly, access is initially limited to approved daybreak red customers through Codex and the API.

102
00:10:58,800 --> 00:11:01,360
But yeah, the race here is pretty clear.

103
00:11:01,646 --> 00:11:08,160
How much software can we secure using these models before these models end up in the hands of people trying to break into it?

104
00:11:08,334 --> 00:11:09,668
That's the whole game right now.

105
00:11:09,840 --> 00:11:22,400
Now, these growing capabilities are also starting to show up in scientific research, which brings us to another interesting update from Anthropic titled Investigating Vault Particles with Claude.

106
00:11:22,606 --> 00:11:25,956
They've apparently been studying these things called vault particles.

107
00:11:26,350 --> 00:11:33,348
Basically, tiny hollow protein shells found in our cells, whose exact purpose scientists still haven't really figured out yet.

108
00:11:33,520 --> 00:11:45,280
And with Claude's help, the team mapped the structure of a related vault from a virus that infects bacteria, with the idea being that studying these simpler versions could help us understand what the human ones actually do.

109
00:11:45,550 --> 00:11:52,740
Claude also helped improve their lab process, cutting the time to prepare samples for microscopy from days to about 90 minutes.

110
00:11:53,166 --> 00:12:02,404
Then it helped process the microscope data, getting a first full structure model ready in just 34 hours, which they estimate would normally have taken them weeks.

111
00:12:02,830 --> 00:12:07,588
So this is just another example of AI speeding up a team's workflow.

112
00:12:07,760 --> 00:12:11,204
In this case, helping scientists test ideas in the lab.

113
00:12:11,470 --> 00:12:24,548
And then on the math side, Axiom released a formalization of the Stacks project, which, if you've never heard of it, is basically a massive, roughly 7,500 page reference work in algebraic geometry.

114
00:12:24,782 --> 00:12:30,960
They have translated it into lean, where a computer can check the proofs, and those results can then be reused in other proofs.

115
00:12:31,342 --> 00:12:40,352
Think of it as turning this huge body of mathematics into a library that people and AI systems can build on, with the logical steps checked automatically.

116
00:12:40,590 --> 00:12:50,404
There's still human review needed to make sure the AI written formalizations faithfully match the original mathematics, but that's a pretty substantial foundation they can now build on.

117
00:12:50,912 --> 00:12:51,792
So yeah.

118
00:12:52,046 --> 00:13:03,232
We've got AI helping build better AI, AI making progress in science and math, and AI getting increasingly capable at finding security flaws in real-world software.

119
00:13:03,438 --> 00:13:07,428
These are pretty much the main areas AI is going ham in right now.

120
00:13:07,712 --> 00:13:22,672
But this brings us to the other big story this week, which I'm sure you've all been following closely, especially my US audience, because the public response to all this is starting to become a real problem, at least for the AI enthusiasts out there.

121
00:13:22,990 --> 00:13:31,312
Democrats just won both chambers of Congress in the US midterms, and voters in communities across the country backed restrictions on data center development.

122
00:13:31,534 --> 00:13:44,292
Congresswoman Yasemin Ansari went further to say pausing advanced AI development altogether and banning artificial superintelligence should be at the top of the Democratic agenda on day one.

123
00:13:44,672 --> 00:13:45,552
So yeah.

124
00:13:45,792 --> 00:13:52,388
And the thing is, while this might sound crazy to some, there is actually substantial public support for this.

125
00:13:52,750 --> 00:13:59,152
According to NBC's exit polling, 76% of voters opposed a new data center being built in their community.

126
00:13:59,312 --> 00:14:01,152
That's about three out of four people.

127
00:14:01,312 --> 00:14:03,632
And that question was about local construction.

128
00:14:03,918 --> 00:14:10,948
So it doesn't mean three out of four people support banning superintelligence, but it shows how much resistance these projects are now facing.

129
00:14:11,246 --> 00:14:19,072
People are genuinely concerned about electricity bills, about water usage, about what their community actually gets out of the deal.

130
00:14:19,312 --> 00:14:24,900
And now those concerns are translating into votes for construction pauses and tighter rules.

131
00:14:25,262 --> 00:14:26,832
Investors are paying attention too.

132
00:14:27,022 --> 00:14:32,912
If you've looked at the markets this week, you already know anything data center related is getting obliterated.

133
00:14:33,166 --> 00:14:35,716
This honestly might be worse than the whole deep seek moment.

134
00:14:36,032 --> 00:14:41,924
So this is actually clearly having a real effect, even though personally I think it's an overreaction.

135
00:14:42,192 --> 00:14:43,524
But obviously, I'm biased.

136
00:14:43,918 --> 00:14:51,392
I should also note that OpenAI actually kind of responded to this by publishing new infrastructure commitments on Thursday.

137
00:14:51,566 --> 00:15:04,112
They say for future campuses it owns and operates, it will always cover the full electricity and infrastructure costs, publish information about water and power use, and give local communities more input.

138
00:15:04,398 --> 00:15:09,284
They're also now calling them compute campuses, if you didn't catch that, not data centers.

139
00:15:09,582 --> 00:15:15,712
So this builds on commitments they'd already been making earlier this year, but you can see why this message matters right now.

140
00:15:15,952 --> 00:15:19,236
They need communities to actually want these projects.

141
00:15:19,472 --> 00:15:26,180
And I guess in a way the pushback happening right now is actually forcing those commitments into becoming a reality, potentially.

142
00:15:26,432 --> 00:15:29,828
And that's really where this whole week comes together for me.

143
00:15:30,032 --> 00:15:37,152
We started with evidence that AI development is accelerating and that AI itself is increasingly contributing to that acceleration.

144
00:15:37,312 --> 00:15:40,352
But those models still run on physical infrastructure.

145
00:15:40,526 --> 00:15:44,272
You need power, you need somewhere to build, and you need permission.

146
00:15:44,462 --> 00:15:56,512
Even if the technology keeps improving, and even if people like Musk are thinking about just moving to space, politics will still have an effect on how fast this build out continues, whether we like it or not.

147
00:15:56,686 --> 00:16:01,348
Personally, I want to see the scientific progress we just talked about continue, of course.

148
00:16:01,582 --> 00:16:09,284
But I also understand why someone looking at a higher electricity bill wants more than a promise that all this will eventually benefit them and their families.

149
00:16:09,614 --> 00:16:14,116
If it were up to me, I would definitely not slow down the build-out of data centers.

150
00:16:14,352 --> 00:16:23,712
But I would certainly force the AI companies to cover the costs and share the wealth with the community, and perhaps just build them in better areas, like Canada.

151
00:16:23,950 --> 00:16:25,988
Obviously, much easier said than done though.

152
00:16:26,192 --> 00:16:27,364
But what do you guys think?

153
00:16:27,630 --> 00:16:33,220
With AI starting to accelerate its own development, is now the time to truly slow things down?

154
00:16:33,472 --> 00:16:38,512
Or should we keep pushing forward and make sure the benefits actually reach everyone?

155
00:16:38,734 --> 00:16:40,356
Obviously, it's more nuanced than that.

156
00:16:40,686 --> 00:16:42,404
But where would you draw the line?

157
00:16:42,734 --> 00:16:43,632
Let me know down below.

158
00:16:43,854 --> 00:16:45,792
And as always, thanks so much for watching.

159
00:16:46,030 --> 00:16:51,232
Feel free to smash that like and subscribe button, and I'll be catching you guys in the next one.

